diff --git a/CLAUDE.md b/CLAUDE.md
index 15692157..1817aee4 100644
--- a/CLAUDE.md
+++ b/CLAUDE.md
@@ -145,6 +145,16 @@ material (only when the user asked). A refused op (nothing under `at`, a
 locked clip, a media window past the file's end) aborts the whole patch unsaved.
 `setProject` also sets `inPoint` / `outPoint` (`null` clears).
 
+A picture-only `updateClip {mediaId}` keeps its recorded audio companions when
+their start, source In and duration still match, including after save/reopen.
+Mixed-source links are never guessed from overlapping clips; `unlinked:true`
+still opts out. Rolling such a cut moves both original-sound channels with the
+picture, preserving their continuous source window. If the paired channels,
+speeds, source handles or locks prevent that, the whole patch is refused; a
+replacement roll never silently applies a smaller delta. Old projects whose
+mixed-source link metadata was already lost need their relationship restored
+explicitly before using this behavior.
+
 Example — cut the 2 s from 10 s to 12 s out of the edit, then crossfade the new cut:
 `{ops:[{op:"extract", from:10, to:12}, {op:"crossfade", at:10, duration:0.5}]}`
 
diff --git a/docs/docs/mcp/index.html b/docs/docs/mcp/index.html
index 0903cd96..08d25025 100644
--- a/docs/docs/mcp/index.html
+++ b/docs/docs/mcp/index.html
@@ -75,7 +75,7 @@
   <article class="docs-main prose">
     <h1>MCP tools</h1>
     <p class="docs-lede">Connect an agent, and the calls it uses to read and change your timeline without burning tokens.</p>
-    <p>Register the MCP server once at user scope as <code class="ic">fablecut</code>, from a clone: <code class="ic">claude mcp add -s user fablecut -- node "&lt;path-to&gt;/MontClip/mcp-server.js"</code>. (MontClip is not published to npm; see <code class="ic">docs/decisions/2026-11-02-brand-montclip.md</code>.) Every Claude Code session then has these tools:</p><ul><li><code class="ic">fablecut_status</code> - auto-starts the editor server, returns URL + project summary. Call first.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_docs</code> - returns this document (<code class="ic">section: "…"</code> returns only matching <code class="ic">## </code> sections).</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code> / <code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> - read / replace the timeline JSON. <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project {compact:true}</code> returns a one-line-per-clip summary instead.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_patch_project</code> - apply targeted ops (add/update/remove clip/media, set project fields, add and open sequences) without round-tripping the document. <strong>Prefer this for edits.</strong></li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_import_media</code> - copy a local file (or download an <code class="ic">https://</code> URL) into <code class="ic">./media/</code> and register it. The stored <code class="ic">src</code> is always <code class="ic">/media/…</code>. <code class="ic">link:true</code> hard-links a local file on the same drive instead of copying it (a several-GB recording imports at once; another drive falls back to a copy).</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_analyze_reference</code> - turn a reference video into an edit blueprint (shots, beats, BPM, energy, drop) + extract its music. See <a href="../remake/">Remake a reference video</a>.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_frame</code> - <strong>look at</strong> a moment and get back an actual image. <code class="ic">{path, t}</code> returns one frame of a source clip; <code class="ic">{path, frames}</code> returns a contact sheet across it; with no <code class="ic">path</code>, <code class="ic">{time}</code> renders the composed timeline. See <a href="../frames/">Look at the footage</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_encode_profiles</code> - list the <strong>delivery presets</strong> (YouTube, Shorts, Reels, TikTok, X, LinkedIn, Master, Broadcast, Web, Archive, saved ones) and the raw ffmpeg <strong>encoding profiles</strong> from <code class="ic">encoding-profiles.json</code>. <code class="ic">{preset:"youtube"}</code> returns one preset's spec. See <a href="../export/">Export</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_normalize_audio</code> - measure clips (BS.1770 LUFS or sample peak, via ffmpeg) and set their clip gain to a loudness target. See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_auto_duck</code> - find where the voice tracks speak and write <code class="ic">duck</code> keyframes that dip the music under it. See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_frame</code> - <strong>see</strong> a moment. <code class="ic">{path, t}</code> → one JPEG frame of a source clip; <code class="ic">{path, frames:12}</code> → a contact sheet across it; <code class="ic">{time}</code> → the composed timeline, graded, as export renders it. Returns a real image, not numbers. See <a href="../frames/">Look at the footage</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_denoise</code> - reduce background noise on audio clips (renders a cleaned copy of the file and switches the clip's stems to it). See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_silence</code> - find the pauses in speech and, with <code class="ic">cut:true</code>, cut them out (extract on the targeted tracks, stems in step). See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_beats</code> - find a music clip's tempo, beat and drop; mark the beats, snap the cuts to them, or re-cut a track's clips on the beat. See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_reframe</code> - reframe for another shape (9:16, 1:1, 4:5): a copy of the sequence on that canvas whose shots follow the subject. See <a href="../sequences/">Sequences</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_highlights</code> - the best moments of long footage cut into a short edit, in a new sequence. See <a href="../sequences/">Sequences</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_audio_track</code> - a recording with several audio tracks (OBS: microphone, game, music…): list them, cut a few seconds of each to listen to, pick the streamer's voice. See <a href="../media/">Media management</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_songs</code> - where each song in a long recording starts and ends, from the sound alone, with the guessed spots marked; kept in the project. See <a href="../export/">Songs in a recording</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_transcribe</code> - turn speech into words with times (on this computer: sherpa-onnx Paraformer zh), read them on the timeline, find a phrase and cut it out. See <a href="../transcripts/">Transcripts and text-based editing</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_lyrics</code> - line up lyrics the user pasted with the singing (on this computer, nothing sent): a proposal only, each line <code class="ic">{text, start, end, matched, confidence}</code> with <code class="ic">n</code> and <code class="ic">k</code>. <code class="ic">fablecut_lyrics_apply {proposalId}</code> then asks the user on the editor's card and, if they agree, puts the lines on a new caption track (unmatched lines grey), one undo step. It never names a song.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_captions</code> - caption clips from the transcript, timed word by word (subtitle, karaoke, highlight, word pop); export or import SRT / WebVTT. See <a href="../captions/">Captions</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_export</code> - render the timeline to a file in <code class="ic">exports/</code> with the editor's own Fast export (open tab, or headless Chrome / Edge). See <a href="../export/">Export</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_scopes</code> - measure the graded picture at a moment (levels, clipping, colour cast), as the editor's scopes see it; also what a grade layer or a clip's masks select. See <a href="../color/">Color</a> and <a href="../masks/">Masks</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_track</code> - motion tracking: follow a region of a video clip, store it as a track, and make a mask or another clip follow it. See <a href="../tracking/">Tracking</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_repair_media</code> - make a video MontClip can cut: a recording cut off mid-way, or any format the editor can't cut (FLV too). A new MP4 replaces the entry; a background job. See <a href="../media/">Media management</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_proxy</code> - make, check or delete proxies: light copies of heavy footage the editor plays while editing (export always reads the originals). See <a href="../media/">Media management</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_projects</code> - list, open, create (from a canvas or a saved template), duplicate, rename, annotate, archive and delete projects. <code class="ic">./project.json</code> is always the open one. See <a href="../projects/">Projects</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_versions</code> - a project's saved versions: save one before big edits, compare a version with now, restore one (never destructive), mark the final cut. See <a href="../versions/">Versions</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_danmaku</code> - import a Bilibili recording's danmaku XML (录播姬 or Bilibili's own) for one recording, list or remove them. See "Danmaku (Bilibili recordings)" below.</li></ul><p>Long speech/song/highlight calls can use <code class="ic">wait_ms</code> (0–15000, default 6000 in Cindy). The same tool returns a durable <code class="ic">taskId</code>, <code class="ic">status</code> and <code class="ic">progress</code>; retrieve the result with <code class="ic">{taskId, wait_ms}</code> in the originating project. Do not busy-poll. Supply <code class="ic">idempotency_key</code> for modifications: retry the same key and arguments to retrieve the original result, use a new key for a new intended edit. With bounded calls and no explicit key, identical arguments reuse the previous task. Completed results survive worker restart; an interrupted mutation is reported as interrupted and is never automatically repeated. Receipts are local files in <code class="ic">analysis/tool-tasks/</code>. Completion is reported only after its receipt is saved. A permanent receipt-write failure returns <code class="ic">TASK_RECEIPT_SAVE_FAILED</code> without replaying the edit; another process may still see the last durable running state while that owner remains alive. Task replies omit the internal session scope and process id. Error text and error metadata pass through one privacy boundary before storage and return. Legacy terminal receipts (including warnings in successful envelopes) are scrubbed on disk before replay, without rerunning any edit. Saved paths under the data root remain relative; outside-root error path fields are omitted or null rather than presented as usable local files.</p><p>Transcription saves completed paragraphs to <code class="ic">analysis/transcripts/</code> before the next paragraph. A transcription <code class="ic">timeout</code> stops its editor job and reports incomplete work with data-root-relative saved transcript paths, using forward slashes. The <code class="ic">saved</code> field and incomplete-work notices never include the local absolute data root; local clients resolve them themselves. With no completed paragraph the notice names <code class="ic">analysis/transcripts/</code> without claiming a file exists. Direct and bounded calls use the same notice. This timeout is separate from <code class="ic">wait_ms</code>, which only limits how long the caller waits and does not stop the background work. A worker exit can interrupt remaining work; saved paragraphs remain. No model is downloaded automatically.</p><h2 id="token-efficient-editing">Token-efficient editing <span class="h-note">important for agents</span><a class="anchor" href="#token-efficient-editing" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p>Editing via full get→modify→set costs thousands of tokens per change. Cheaper:</p><ol><li><strong>Plan</strong> from <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project {compact:true}</code> (≈10× smaller than the JSON) and <code class="ic">fablecut_status</code> - fetch the full JSON only to inspect exact keyframes.</li><li><strong>Edit</strong> with <code class="ic">fablecut_patch_project</code> ops - send only what changes, e.g. <code class="ic">{ops:[{op:"updateClip", id:"c_v2", set:{props:{filterPreset:"noir"}}}]}</code>. It re-reads the latest document internally, so it is merge-safe by design (no CONFLICT dance) and never destroys concurrent UI tweaks. To also be sure the clips you touch haven't changed since you looked, pass <code class="ic">baseRevision</code> (the <code class="ic">revision</code> you planned from - <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code>, <code class="ic">fablecut_status</code> or the last "Patched (revision N)" result): if the project has moved past it, the patch is refused with "CONFLICT - not saved" and the current revision. Re-check the clips, then retry with that revision.</li><li><strong>Docs</strong>: request <code class="ic">fablecut_docs {section:"schema"}</code> (or <a href="../recipes/">Recipes</a>, "Remake", …) instead of the whole manual; skip it entirely if the schema is already in context.</li><li><strong>Media questions</strong> (duration, resolution, fps, codec, audio channels, file size, how often it is used): read them from the registered media entries or the compact view's MEDIA lines - don't shell out to ffprobe; the editor probes every file and writes them back (see <a href="../media/">Media management</a>). For what the footage <em>looks like</em>, use <code class="ic">fablecut_frame</code> - it is far cheaper than reading a file, and far more reliable than inferring from a number.</li><li>Batch related changes into ONE patch call (ops apply in order, one revision bump).</li><li><strong>Cut and trim with the edit ops</strong> (next section) rather than recomputing <code class="ic">start</code> / <code class="ic">in</code> / <code class="ic">duration</code> by hand - they keep linked stems in sync.</li></ol><h2 id="timeline-edit-ops">Timeline edit ops <span class="h-note"><code class="ic">fablecut_patch_project</code></span><a class="anchor" href="#timeline-edit-ops" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p>The editor's own split / ripple / trim code (<code class="ic">edit-ops.js</code>), run on the document - so linked audio stems ride along, edits land on the <strong>targeted</strong> tracks (<code class="ic">untargetedTracks</code>, see Semantics), and locked clips stay put, exactly as with the keyboard shortcuts. Times are seconds.</p><div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>op</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"split", at, ids?}</code></td><td>cut at <code class="ic">at</code> - the named clips (and their stems), or every targeted track</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"rippleDelete", ids}</code></td><td>remove clips; later clips on those tracks close the hole</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"closeGap", at}</code></td><td>close the gap under <code class="ic">at</code> on every targeted track</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"lift" | "extract", from?, to?}</code></td><td>remove a range (default: <code class="ic">inPoint</code>→<code class="ic">outPoint</code>, which then clear); extract also closes it</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"insert" | "overwrite", mediaId, at, in?, duration?}</code></td><td>three-point edit: place <code class="ic">in</code>→<code class="ic">in+duration</code> of the media at <code class="ic">at</code> (default: the rest of the media). Insert pushes later clips right; overwrite replaces what is under it. A video brings one linked audio stem per channel.</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"rippleTrim", id, side:"in"|"out", delta}</code></td><td>move a head / tail; everything after follows</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"roll", id, side, delta}</code></td><td>move the cut between two clips; nothing else moves</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"slip", id, delta}</code> · <code class="ic">{op:"slide", id, delta}</code></td><td>new source window in place · move a clip between its neighbors</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"crossfade", ids? | at, duration?}</code></td><td>constant-power audio crossfade at the cuts touching <code class="ic">ids</code>, or the cut nearest <code class="ic">at</code> (handles borrowed from both sides)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Trims are clamped to the source media, the free room and <code class="ic">MIN_DUR</code>; the result note shows the applied delta (<code class="ic">… (asked +5, clamped)</code>). Any edit op takes <code class="ic">tracks:[…]</code> to target lanes for that op only, and <code class="ic">force:true</code> to edit locked material (only when the user asked). A refused op (nothing under <code class="ic">at</code>, a locked clip, a media window past the file's end) aborts the whole patch unsaved. <code class="ic">setProject</code> also sets <code class="ic">inPoint</code> / <code class="ic">outPoint</code> (<code class="ic">null</code> clears).</p><p>Example - cut the 2 s from 10 s to 12 s out of the edit, then crossfade the new cut: <code class="ic">{ops:[{op:"extract", from:10, to:12}, {op:"crossfade", at:10, duration:0.5}]}</code></p><p><strong><code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> is conflict-checked.</strong> The MCP server remembers the <code class="ic">revision</code> from the most recent <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code> call. If <code class="ic">project.json</code> has been written by anyone else since that read (e.g. the user dragged a clip in the UI), <code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> refuses with a "CONFLICT - not saved" error instead of overwriting. Protocol:</p><ol><li><code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code> → read the document and note its <code class="ic">revision</code>.</li><li>Apply your edits in memory, bump <code class="ic">revision</code>.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> → if it succeeds you're done.</li><li><strong>On conflict</strong>: call <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code> again to get the latest document, re-apply your intended changes on top of it, bump <code class="ic">revision</code>, and call <code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> again.</li></ol><p>Pass <code class="ic">force: true</code> to <code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> only when the user explicitly asks to overwrite conflicting changes. <code class="ic">fablecut_import_media</code> only appends a new media entry and always merges safely - no conflict check needed.</p><p>For Claude Desktop, add to its MCP config: <code class="ic">{"mcpServers":{"fablecut":{"command":"node","args":["&lt;path-to&gt;/MontClip/mcp-server.js"]}}}</code> Direct file editing of <code class="ic">project.json</code> (below) works too and is equivalent.</p><h2 id="where-the-files-are">Where the files are<a class="anchor" href="#where-the-files-are" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p><code class="ic">project.json</code>, <code class="ic">projects/</code>, <code class="ic">media/</code>, <code class="ic">exports/</code>, <code class="ic">analysis/</code> and <code class="ic">library/</code> normally sit in the repo next to <code class="ic">server.js</code>; when the code sits under <code class="ic">node_modules</code> they sit in <code class="ic">~/FableCut</code>. Set <strong><code class="ic">FABLECUT_DATA_DIR</code></strong> to move all six somewhere else; the code and the static app files stay in the install directory either way. The plugin sets this so a plugin update can replace the install directory without touching anyone's timeline or footage. <strong>Don't assume <code class="ic">project.json</code> is beside <code class="ic">mcp-server.js</code></strong> - call <code class="ic">fablecut_status</code>, which reports the real paths.</p><p><strong>One engine writes a data folder.</strong> The first <code class="ic">server.js</code> to start holds <code class="ic">&lt;data dir&gt;/.montclip-writer.lock</code> (<code class="ic">{pid, shell, port, started}</code>; <code class="ic">shell</code> is <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_SHELL</code>, else <code class="ic">desktop</code> or <code class="ic">server</code>). Another one started on the same folder - the Windows app and the Cindy plugin pointed at one folder, say - refuses to start and names the first. A lock whose process is gone is taken over.</p><p><strong>Embedded in Cindy</strong> (the MontClip-Cindy plugin, see its README) the engine runs with <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_HOST=cindy</code> and a fresh 64-hex <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_EMBED_TOKEN</code>, bound to <code class="ic">127.0.0.1</code> only (anything else, or desktop mode, refuses to start). It answers only requests that carry the token: as <code class="ic">X-MontClip-Session</code> (the plugin worker, and <code class="ic">mcp-server.js</code> when it runs with the same two variables), or as the <code class="ic">montclip_embed</code> cookie (HttpOnly, <code class="ic">SameSite=None; Secure; Partitioned</code>), which a page load (GET) of any URL with <code class="ic">?token=&lt;token&gt;</code> receives together with a 302 to the same URL without it. The cookie is not the token: each such page load gets its own random session, which the server remembers (the newest 64) and can end on its own. Everything else gets 403. Every answer says <code class="ic">Referrer-Policy: no-referrer</code>, and the token is never put in a redirect, a log line or an error. A headless export or measurement browser gets a one-time ticket (one use, one minute) in its URL instead of the token. Without <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_HOST=cindy</code> none of this applies. The plugin downloads FFmpeg itself and sets <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_FFMPEG_REMEDY</code> to the advice <code class="ic">ffmpeg-path.js</code> adds when that copy is missing (unset or blank: "Reinstall MontClip to restore it.", the Windows app's advice).</p><p><strong>Which task an embedded editor belongs to</strong> (MontClip-Cindy#18, QQ's option ①) is bound to its event-stream connection, never to the cookie: one Cindy page frames every task's editor, and they share one cookie jar (the cookie only says "an embedded editor"). The worker registers a ticket for a task, <code class="ic">POST /api/embed/ticket {project, session}</code> (worker only) → <code class="ic">{ticket, expiresInMs}</code>: 256 random bits, one use, 60 s. The parent page puts it once in the frame's first address, <code class="ic">?host=cindy&amp;ticket=&lt;ticket&gt;</code>. The page takes it out of its address at once and hands it in with the id its event stream got (SSE event <code class="ic">conn</code>), <code class="ic">POST /api/embed/claim {ticket, conn}</code>; that one connection is bound to the task, in memory, until it closes. A reused, expired, unknown (another engine's) ticket, a connection that is not open or is already bound: 403 <code class="ic">TASK_TICKET_REFUSED</code>, nothing bound. When the stream reconnects, the page posts <code class="ic">{type:"montclip-need-ticket"}</code> to its parent and takes <code class="ic">{type:"montclip-host-ticket", ticket}</code> only from the parent window and origin. <code class="ic">POST /api/ui/command</code> is the worker's only; with <code class="ic">{session, project?}</code> it goes to that task's bound connections alone (none: 409 <code class="ic">NO_EDITOR</code> at once), and the answer is taken by its one-time command id, never by what the page says about itself. Error text drops <code class="ic">ticket=</code> / <code class="ic">token=</code> (tool-error.js, problem-redact.js); the engine keeps no access log.</p><p><strong>The side panel and its task's editor</strong> (MontClip#215 E3) talk over <code class="ic">BroadcastChannel("montclip-editor:&lt;channel&gt;")</code>. Every page of the engine's origin shares channel names, so <code class="ic">&lt;channel&gt;</code> is the task's own: 32 hex of an HMAC-SHA256 of <code class="ic">project + "\0" + session</code> under a key drawn when the engine starts (not the embed token; a restart changes it). The claim answers <code class="ic">{bound: true, channel}</code>; the panel trades its own task ticket with <code class="ic">POST /api/embed/channel {ticket}</code> → <code class="ic">{channel}</code>, which binds no connection and gets no UI commands (refused like a claim: 403 <code class="ic">TASK_TICKET_REFUSED</code>). No channel, no BroadcastChannel: the editor never falls back to a shared name. The editor sends <code class="ic">{type:"editor-state", time, playing, selId, revision}</code> on a change and at least once a second, and takes <code class="ic">{type:"seek", t}</code> and <code class="ic">{type:"hello"}</code> only; messages carry no project, session, ticket or channel. The channel stays in the page's memory; a <code class="ic">channel</code> key, and a 32-hex value after <code class="ic">channel=</code> / <code class="ic">channel:</code>, are dropped from error text like a ticket.</p><p>The editor page opened with <code class="ic">?host=cindy</code> (<code class="ic">host-embed.js</code>) hides its built-in assistant (Cindy's chat takes its place) and keeps it from sending anything: it reads no service key, reaches no AI service and sends no message (the desktop main process refuses its requests from such a page too); an approval it would ask for is 「不同意」 at once, with code <code class="ic">HOST_EMBEDDED</code>. The lyrics card is the page's own dialog and still asks. The editor follows Cindy's theme: <code class="ic">?theme=light|dark</code> at load (any other value is ignored), then <code class="ic">postMessage({type:"montclip-host-theme", scheme})</code>, taken only when it comes from the framing page: <code class="ic">event.source</code> is <code class="ic">window.parent</code> and <code class="ic">event.origin</code> is that page's origin (<code class="ic">location.ancestorOrigins</code>, else the referrer; when neither names one, no message is taken).</p><p><code class="ic">mcp-server.js</code> run as a program speaks MCP on stdio; required as a module (the Cindy plugin's Node worker does) it only hands over <code class="ic">{TOOLS, callTool, ensureUIServer}</code> and leaves stdin alone. <code class="ic">callTool(name, args, {signal})</code> takes an <code class="ic">AbortSignal</code> for the host giving up on a call: a job the call started on the editor's queue (export, scope reading, timeline frame, tracking, transcription) is cancelled then. On stdio, <code class="ic">notifications/cancelled</code> and SIGTERM / SIGINT do the same.</p><p>Tests (and nothing else) may set <strong><code class="ic">FABLECUT_NO_FS_WATCH=1</code></strong> to skip <code class="ic">fs.watch</code>. On Windows, libuv can abort the process when a file is created under a temp data dir. Production leaves watching on so the UI live-reloads. Tests may also set <strong><code class="ic">FABLECUT_NO_AUTOSTART=1</code></strong> so <code class="ic">ensureUIServer()</code> reports a missing editor server instead of spawning one - the auto-start is detached, so a test that triggers it would leave a <code class="ic">server.js</code> running after it finishes. Tests may set <strong><code class="ic">MONTCLIP_EXPORT_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS</code></strong> to change how long the export's self-check may spend reading back one clip's file (default 20000), standing in for a slow disk. A read that runs out of time is a warning on a kept file, never a failed export (<code class="ic">EXPORT_CLIP_UNCHECKED</code>; a file that really can't be read still fails it, <code class="ic">EXPORT_AUDIO_FAILED</code>).</p>
+    <p>Register the MCP server once at user scope as <code class="ic">fablecut</code>, from a clone: <code class="ic">claude mcp add -s user fablecut -- node "&lt;path-to&gt;/MontClip/mcp-server.js"</code>. (MontClip is not published to npm; see <code class="ic">docs/decisions/2026-11-02-brand-montclip.md</code>.) Every Claude Code session then has these tools:</p><ul><li><code class="ic">fablecut_status</code> - auto-starts the editor server, returns URL + project summary. Call first.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_docs</code> - returns this document (<code class="ic">section: "…"</code> returns only matching <code class="ic">## </code> sections).</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code> / <code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> - read / replace the timeline JSON. <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project {compact:true}</code> returns a one-line-per-clip summary instead.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_patch_project</code> - apply targeted ops (add/update/remove clip/media, set project fields, add and open sequences) without round-tripping the document. <strong>Prefer this for edits.</strong></li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_import_media</code> - copy a local file (or download an <code class="ic">https://</code> URL) into <code class="ic">./media/</code> and register it. The stored <code class="ic">src</code> is always <code class="ic">/media/…</code>. <code class="ic">link:true</code> hard-links a local file on the same drive instead of copying it (a several-GB recording imports at once; another drive falls back to a copy).</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_analyze_reference</code> - turn a reference video into an edit blueprint (shots, beats, BPM, energy, drop) + extract its music. See <a href="../remake/">Remake a reference video</a>.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_frame</code> - <strong>look at</strong> a moment and get back an actual image. <code class="ic">{path, t}</code> returns one frame of a source clip; <code class="ic">{path, frames}</code> returns a contact sheet across it; with no <code class="ic">path</code>, <code class="ic">{time}</code> renders the composed timeline. See <a href="../frames/">Look at the footage</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_encode_profiles</code> - list the <strong>delivery presets</strong> (YouTube, Shorts, Reels, TikTok, X, LinkedIn, Master, Broadcast, Web, Archive, saved ones) and the raw ffmpeg <strong>encoding profiles</strong> from <code class="ic">encoding-profiles.json</code>. <code class="ic">{preset:"youtube"}</code> returns one preset's spec. See <a href="../export/">Export</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_normalize_audio</code> - measure clips (BS.1770 LUFS or sample peak, via ffmpeg) and set their clip gain to a loudness target. See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_auto_duck</code> - find where the voice tracks speak and write <code class="ic">duck</code> keyframes that dip the music under it. See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_frame</code> - <strong>see</strong> a moment. <code class="ic">{path, t}</code> → one JPEG frame of a source clip; <code class="ic">{path, frames:12}</code> → a contact sheet across it; <code class="ic">{time}</code> → the composed timeline, graded, as export renders it. Returns a real image, not numbers. See <a href="../frames/">Look at the footage</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_denoise</code> - reduce background noise on audio clips (renders a cleaned copy of the file and switches the clip's stems to it). See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_silence</code> - find the pauses in speech and, with <code class="ic">cut:true</code>, cut them out (extract on the targeted tracks, stems in step). See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_beats</code> - find a music clip's tempo, beat and drop; mark the beats, snap the cuts to them, or re-cut a track's clips on the beat. See <a href="../audio/">Audio mix</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_reframe</code> - reframe for another shape (9:16, 1:1, 4:5): a copy of the sequence on that canvas whose shots follow the subject. See <a href="../sequences/">Sequences</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_highlights</code> - the best moments of long footage cut into a short edit, in a new sequence. See <a href="../sequences/">Sequences</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_audio_track</code> - a recording with several audio tracks (OBS: microphone, game, music…): list them, cut a few seconds of each to listen to, pick the streamer's voice. See <a href="../media/">Media management</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_songs</code> - where each song in a long recording starts and ends, from the sound alone, with the guessed spots marked; kept in the project. See <a href="../export/">Songs in a recording</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_transcribe</code> - turn speech into words with times (on this computer: sherpa-onnx Paraformer zh), read them on the timeline, find a phrase and cut it out. See <a href="../transcripts/">Transcripts and text-based editing</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_lyrics</code> - line up lyrics the user pasted with the singing (on this computer, nothing sent): a proposal only, each line <code class="ic">{text, start, end, matched, confidence}</code> with <code class="ic">n</code> and <code class="ic">k</code>. <code class="ic">fablecut_lyrics_apply {proposalId}</code> then asks the user on the editor's card and, if they agree, puts the lines on a new caption track (unmatched lines grey), one undo step. It never names a song.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_captions</code> - caption clips from the transcript, timed word by word (subtitle, karaoke, highlight, word pop); export or import SRT / WebVTT. See <a href="../captions/">Captions</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_export</code> - render the timeline to a file in <code class="ic">exports/</code> with the editor's own Fast export (open tab, or headless Chrome / Edge). See <a href="../export/">Export</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_scopes</code> - measure the graded picture at a moment (levels, clipping, colour cast), as the editor's scopes see it; also what a grade layer or a clip's masks select. See <a href="../color/">Color</a> and <a href="../masks/">Masks</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_track</code> - motion tracking: follow a region of a video clip, store it as a track, and make a mask or another clip follow it. See <a href="../tracking/">Tracking</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_repair_media</code> - make a video MontClip can cut: a recording cut off mid-way, or any format the editor can't cut (FLV too). A new MP4 replaces the entry; a background job. See <a href="../media/">Media management</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_proxy</code> - make, check or delete proxies: light copies of heavy footage the editor plays while editing (export always reads the originals). See <a href="../media/">Media management</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_projects</code> - list, open, create (from a canvas or a saved template), duplicate, rename, annotate, archive and delete projects. <code class="ic">./project.json</code> is always the open one. See <a href="../projects/">Projects</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_versions</code> - a project's saved versions: save one before big edits, compare a version with now, restore one (never destructive), mark the final cut. See <a href="../versions/">Versions</a> below.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_danmaku</code> - import a Bilibili recording's danmaku XML (录播姬 or Bilibili's own) for one recording, list or remove them. See "Danmaku (Bilibili recordings)" below.</li></ul><p>Long speech/song/highlight calls can use <code class="ic">wait_ms</code> (0–15000, default 6000 in Cindy). The same tool returns a durable <code class="ic">taskId</code>, <code class="ic">status</code> and <code class="ic">progress</code>; retrieve the result with <code class="ic">{taskId, wait_ms}</code> in the originating project. Do not busy-poll. Supply <code class="ic">idempotency_key</code> for modifications: retry the same key and arguments to retrieve the original result, use a new key for a new intended edit. With bounded calls and no explicit key, identical arguments reuse the previous task. Completed results survive worker restart; an interrupted mutation is reported as interrupted and is never automatically repeated. Receipts are local files in <code class="ic">analysis/tool-tasks/</code>. Completion is reported only after its receipt is saved. A permanent receipt-write failure returns <code class="ic">TASK_RECEIPT_SAVE_FAILED</code> without replaying the edit; another process may still see the last durable running state while that owner remains alive. Task replies omit the internal session scope and process id. Error text and error metadata pass through one privacy boundary before storage and return. Legacy terminal receipts (including warnings in successful envelopes) are scrubbed on disk before replay, without rerunning any edit. Saved paths under the data root remain relative; outside-root error path fields are omitted or null rather than presented as usable local files.</p><p>Transcription saves completed paragraphs to <code class="ic">analysis/transcripts/</code> before the next paragraph. A transcription <code class="ic">timeout</code> stops its editor job and reports incomplete work with data-root-relative saved transcript paths, using forward slashes. The <code class="ic">saved</code> field and incomplete-work notices never include the local absolute data root; local clients resolve them themselves. With no completed paragraph the notice names <code class="ic">analysis/transcripts/</code> without claiming a file exists. Direct and bounded calls use the same notice. This timeout is separate from <code class="ic">wait_ms</code>, which only limits how long the caller waits and does not stop the background work. A worker exit can interrupt remaining work; saved paragraphs remain. No model is downloaded automatically.</p><h2 id="token-efficient-editing">Token-efficient editing <span class="h-note">important for agents</span><a class="anchor" href="#token-efficient-editing" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p>Editing via full get→modify→set costs thousands of tokens per change. Cheaper:</p><ol><li><strong>Plan</strong> from <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project {compact:true}</code> (≈10× smaller than the JSON) and <code class="ic">fablecut_status</code> - fetch the full JSON only to inspect exact keyframes.</li><li><strong>Edit</strong> with <code class="ic">fablecut_patch_project</code> ops - send only what changes, e.g. <code class="ic">{ops:[{op:"updateClip", id:"c_v2", set:{props:{filterPreset:"noir"}}}]}</code>. It re-reads the latest document internally, so it is merge-safe by design (no CONFLICT dance) and never destroys concurrent UI tweaks. To also be sure the clips you touch haven't changed since you looked, pass <code class="ic">baseRevision</code> (the <code class="ic">revision</code> you planned from - <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code>, <code class="ic">fablecut_status</code> or the last "Patched (revision N)" result): if the project has moved past it, the patch is refused with "CONFLICT - not saved" and the current revision. Re-check the clips, then retry with that revision.</li><li><strong>Docs</strong>: request <code class="ic">fablecut_docs {section:"schema"}</code> (or <a href="../recipes/">Recipes</a>, "Remake", …) instead of the whole manual; skip it entirely if the schema is already in context.</li><li><strong>Media questions</strong> (duration, resolution, fps, codec, audio channels, file size, how often it is used): read them from the registered media entries or the compact view's MEDIA lines - don't shell out to ffprobe; the editor probes every file and writes them back (see <a href="../media/">Media management</a>). For what the footage <em>looks like</em>, use <code class="ic">fablecut_frame</code> - it is far cheaper than reading a file, and far more reliable than inferring from a number.</li><li>Batch related changes into ONE patch call (ops apply in order, one revision bump).</li><li><strong>Cut and trim with the edit ops</strong> (next section) rather than recomputing <code class="ic">start</code> / <code class="ic">in</code> / <code class="ic">duration</code> by hand - they keep linked stems in sync.</li></ol><h2 id="timeline-edit-ops">Timeline edit ops <span class="h-note"><code class="ic">fablecut_patch_project</code></span><a class="anchor" href="#timeline-edit-ops" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p>The editor's own split / ripple / trim code (<code class="ic">edit-ops.js</code>), run on the document - so linked audio stems ride along, edits land on the <strong>targeted</strong> tracks (<code class="ic">untargetedTracks</code>, see Semantics), and locked clips stay put, exactly as with the keyboard shortcuts. Times are seconds.</p><div class="tbl"><table><thead><tr><th>op</th><th>does</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"split", at, ids?}</code></td><td>cut at <code class="ic">at</code> - the named clips (and their stems), or every targeted track</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"rippleDelete", ids}</code></td><td>remove clips; later clips on those tracks close the hole</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"closeGap", at}</code></td><td>close the gap under <code class="ic">at</code> on every targeted track</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"lift" | "extract", from?, to?}</code></td><td>remove a range (default: <code class="ic">inPoint</code>→<code class="ic">outPoint</code>, which then clear); extract also closes it</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"insert" | "overwrite", mediaId, at, in?, duration?}</code></td><td>three-point edit: place <code class="ic">in</code>→<code class="ic">in+duration</code> of the media at <code class="ic">at</code> (default: the rest of the media). Insert pushes later clips right; overwrite replaces what is under it. A video brings one linked audio stem per channel.</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"rippleTrim", id, side:"in"|"out", delta}</code></td><td>move a head / tail; everything after follows</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"roll", id, side, delta}</code></td><td>move the cut between two clips; nothing else moves</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"slip", id, delta}</code> · <code class="ic">{op:"slide", id, delta}</code></td><td>new source window in place · move a clip between its neighbors</td></tr><tr><td><code class="ic">{op:"crossfade", ids? | at, duration?}</code></td><td>constant-power audio crossfade at the cuts touching <code class="ic">ids</code>, or the cut nearest <code class="ic">at</code> (handles borrowed from both sides)</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Trims are clamped to the source media, the free room and <code class="ic">MIN_DUR</code>; the result note shows the applied delta (<code class="ic">… (asked +5, clamped)</code>). Any edit op takes <code class="ic">tracks:[…]</code> to target lanes for that op only, and <code class="ic">force:true</code> to edit locked material (only when the user asked). A refused op (nothing under <code class="ic">at</code>, a locked clip, a media window past the file's end) aborts the whole patch unsaved. <code class="ic">setProject</code> also sets <code class="ic">inPoint</code> / <code class="ic">outPoint</code> (<code class="ic">null</code> clears).</p><p>A picture-only <code class="ic">updateClip {mediaId}</code> keeps its recorded audio companions when their start, source In and duration still match, including after save/reopen. Mixed-source links are never guessed from overlapping clips; <code class="ic">unlinked:true</code> still opts out. Rolling such a cut moves both original-sound channels with the picture, preserving their continuous source window. If the paired channels, speeds, source handles or locks prevent that, the whole patch is refused; a replacement roll never silently applies a smaller delta. Old projects whose mixed-source link metadata was already lost need their relationship restored explicitly before using this behavior.</p><p>Example - cut the 2 s from 10 s to 12 s out of the edit, then crossfade the new cut: <code class="ic">{ops:[{op:"extract", from:10, to:12}, {op:"crossfade", at:10, duration:0.5}]}</code></p><p><strong><code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> is conflict-checked.</strong> The MCP server remembers the <code class="ic">revision</code> from the most recent <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code> call. If <code class="ic">project.json</code> has been written by anyone else since that read (e.g. the user dragged a clip in the UI), <code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> refuses with a "CONFLICT - not saved" error instead of overwriting. Protocol:</p><ol><li><code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code> → read the document and note its <code class="ic">revision</code>.</li><li>Apply your edits in memory, bump <code class="ic">revision</code>.</li><li><code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> → if it succeeds you're done.</li><li><strong>On conflict</strong>: call <code class="ic">fablecut_get_project</code> again to get the latest document, re-apply your intended changes on top of it, bump <code class="ic">revision</code>, and call <code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> again.</li></ol><p>Pass <code class="ic">force: true</code> to <code class="ic">fablecut_set_project</code> only when the user explicitly asks to overwrite conflicting changes. <code class="ic">fablecut_import_media</code> only appends a new media entry and always merges safely - no conflict check needed.</p><p>For Claude Desktop, add to its MCP config: <code class="ic">{"mcpServers":{"fablecut":{"command":"node","args":["&lt;path-to&gt;/MontClip/mcp-server.js"]}}}</code> Direct file editing of <code class="ic">project.json</code> (below) works too and is equivalent.</p><h2 id="where-the-files-are">Where the files are<a class="anchor" href="#where-the-files-are" aria-label="Link to this section">#</a></h2><p><code class="ic">project.json</code>, <code class="ic">projects/</code>, <code class="ic">media/</code>, <code class="ic">exports/</code>, <code class="ic">analysis/</code> and <code class="ic">library/</code> normally sit in the repo next to <code class="ic">server.js</code>; when the code sits under <code class="ic">node_modules</code> they sit in <code class="ic">~/FableCut</code>. Set <strong><code class="ic">FABLECUT_DATA_DIR</code></strong> to move all six somewhere else; the code and the static app files stay in the install directory either way. The plugin sets this so a plugin update can replace the install directory without touching anyone's timeline or footage. <strong>Don't assume <code class="ic">project.json</code> is beside <code class="ic">mcp-server.js</code></strong> - call <code class="ic">fablecut_status</code>, which reports the real paths.</p><p><strong>One engine writes a data folder.</strong> The first <code class="ic">server.js</code> to start holds <code class="ic">&lt;data dir&gt;/.montclip-writer.lock</code> (<code class="ic">{pid, shell, port, started}</code>; <code class="ic">shell</code> is <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_SHELL</code>, else <code class="ic">desktop</code> or <code class="ic">server</code>). Another one started on the same folder - the Windows app and the Cindy plugin pointed at one folder, say - refuses to start and names the first. A lock whose process is gone is taken over.</p><p><strong>Embedded in Cindy</strong> (the MontClip-Cindy plugin, see its README) the engine runs with <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_HOST=cindy</code> and a fresh 64-hex <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_EMBED_TOKEN</code>, bound to <code class="ic">127.0.0.1</code> only (anything else, or desktop mode, refuses to start). It answers only requests that carry the token: as <code class="ic">X-MontClip-Session</code> (the plugin worker, and <code class="ic">mcp-server.js</code> when it runs with the same two variables), or as the <code class="ic">montclip_embed</code> cookie (HttpOnly, <code class="ic">SameSite=None; Secure; Partitioned</code>), which a page load (GET) of any URL with <code class="ic">?token=&lt;token&gt;</code> receives together with a 302 to the same URL without it. The cookie is not the token: each such page load gets its own random session, which the server remembers (the newest 64) and can end on its own. Everything else gets 403. Every answer says <code class="ic">Referrer-Policy: no-referrer</code>, and the token is never put in a redirect, a log line or an error. A headless export or measurement browser gets a one-time ticket (one use, one minute) in its URL instead of the token. Without <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_HOST=cindy</code> none of this applies. The plugin downloads FFmpeg itself and sets <code class="ic">MONTCLIP_FFMPEG_REMEDY</code> to the advice <code class="ic">ffmpeg-path.js</code> adds when that copy is missing (unset or blank: "Reinstall MontClip to restore it.", the Windows app's advice).</p><p><strong>Which task an embedded editor belongs to</strong> (MontClip-Cindy#18, QQ's option ①) is bound to its event-stream connection, never to the cookie: one Cindy page frames every task's editor, and they share one cookie jar (the cookie only says "an embedded editor"). The worker registers a ticket for a task, <code class="ic">POST /api/embed/ticket {project, session}</code> (worker only) → <code class="ic">{ticket, expiresInMs}</code>: 256 random bits, one use, 60 s. The parent page puts it once in the frame's first address, <code class="ic">?host=cindy&amp;ticket=&lt;ticket&gt;</code>. The page takes it out of its address at once and hands it in with the id its event stream got (SSE event <code class="ic">conn</code>), <code class="ic">POST /api/embed/claim {ticket, conn}</code>; that one connection is bound to the task, in memory, until it closes. A reused, expired, unknown (another engine's) ticket, a connection that is not open or is already bound: 403 <code class="ic">TASK_TICKET_REFUSED</code>, nothing bound. When the stream reconnects, the page posts <code class="ic">{type:"montclip-need-ticket"}</code> to its parent and takes <code class="ic">{type:"montclip-host-ticket", ticket}</code> only from the parent window and origin. <code class="ic">POST /api/ui/command</code> is the worker's only; with <code class="ic">{session, project?}</code> it goes to that task's bound connections alone (none: 409 <code class="ic">NO_EDITOR</code> at once), and the answer is taken by its one-time command id, never by what the page says about itself. Error text drops <code class="ic">ticket=</code> / <code class="ic">token=</code> (tool-error.js, problem-redact.js); the engine keeps no access log.</p><p><strong>The side panel and its task's editor</strong> (MontClip#215 E3) talk over <code class="ic">BroadcastChannel("montclip-editor:&lt;channel&gt;")</code>. Every page of the engine's origin shares channel names, so <code class="ic">&lt;channel&gt;</code> is the task's own: 32 hex of an HMAC-SHA256 of <code class="ic">project + "\0" + session</code> under a key drawn when the engine starts (not the embed token; a restart changes it). The claim answers <code class="ic">{bound: true, channel}</code>; the panel trades its own task ticket with <code class="ic">POST /api/embed/channel {ticket}</code> → <code class="ic">{channel}</code>, which binds no connection and gets no UI commands (refused like a claim: 403 <code class="ic">TASK_TICKET_REFUSED</code>). No channel, no BroadcastChannel: the editor never falls back to a shared name. The editor sends <code class="ic">{type:"editor-state", time, playing, selId, revision}</code> on a change and at least once a second, and takes <code class="ic">{type:"seek", t}</code> and <code class="ic">{type:"hello"}</code> only; messages carry no project, session, ticket or channel. The channel stays in the page's memory; a <code class="ic">channel</code> key, and a 32-hex value after <code class="ic">channel=</code> / <code class="ic">channel:</code>, are dropped from error text like a ticket.</p><p>The editor page opened with <code class="ic">?host=cindy</code> (<code class="ic">host-embed.js</code>) hides its built-in assistant (Cindy's chat takes its place) and keeps it from sending anything: it reads no service key, reaches no AI service and sends no message (the desktop main process refuses its requests from such a page too); an approval it would ask for is 「不同意」 at once, with code <code class="ic">HOST_EMBEDDED</code>. The lyrics card is the page's own dialog and still asks. The editor follows Cindy's theme: <code class="ic">?theme=light|dark</code> at load (any other value is ignored), then <code class="ic">postMessage({type:"montclip-host-theme", scheme})</code>, taken only when it comes from the framing page: <code class="ic">event.source</code> is <code class="ic">window.parent</code> and <code class="ic">event.origin</code> is that page's origin (<code class="ic">location.ancestorOrigins</code>, else the referrer; when neither names one, no message is taken).</p><p><code class="ic">mcp-server.js</code> run as a program speaks MCP on stdio; required as a module (the Cindy plugin's Node worker does) it only hands over <code class="ic">{TOOLS, callTool, ensureUIServer}</code> and leaves stdin alone. <code class="ic">callTool(name, args, {signal})</code> takes an <code class="ic">AbortSignal</code> for the host giving up on a call: a job the call started on the editor's queue (export, scope reading, timeline frame, tracking, transcription) is cancelled then. On stdio, <code class="ic">notifications/cancelled</code> and SIGTERM / SIGINT do the same.</p><p>Tests (and nothing else) may set <strong><code class="ic">FABLECUT_NO_FS_WATCH=1</code></strong> to skip <code class="ic">fs.watch</code>. On Windows, libuv can abort the process when a file is created under a temp data dir. Production leaves watching on so the UI live-reloads. Tests may also set <strong><code class="ic">FABLECUT_NO_AUTOSTART=1</code></strong> so <code class="ic">ensureUIServer()</code> reports a missing editor server instead of spawning one - the auto-start is detached, so a test that triggers it would leave a <code class="ic">server.js</code> running after it finishes. Tests may set <strong><code class="ic">MONTCLIP_EXPORT_CHECK_TIMEOUT_MS</code></strong> to change how long the export's self-check may spend reading back one clip's file (default 20000), standing in for a slow disk. A read that runs out of time is a warning on a kept file, never a failed export (<code class="ic">EXPORT_CLIP_UNCHECKED</code>; a file that really can't be read still fails it, <code class="ic">EXPORT_AUDIO_FAILED</code>).</p>
